  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday... \n\nWe'll see a brief lull in precipitation before showers ramp up again \nlate tonight/early Friday morning. While a few rumbles of thunder \ncan't be ruled out Friday afternoon and evening, overall instability \nwill remain weak at best. Substantial Gulf moisture will remain over \nthe Central CONUS, with PWATs rising to 1.5\"-2\". LREF probs estimate \na 60-80% chance of seeing more than 1.5\" through Saturday and a 35-\n50% chance of seeing greater than 2.5\" of rain.  \n\nCloud cover and showers will keep Friday's temperatures cool, in the \nlow to mid 60s. Precipitation chances wind down Saturday afternoon \nand evening, ending from west to east. Temperatures Saturday \nafternoon will be dependent on how quickly cloud cover clears out of \nthe region. If afternoon sun manages to peak through, highs will \nlikely reach into the mid 70s. However if cloud cover prevails, \ntemperatures will remain cooler, in the 60s to lower 70s. \n\nSunday and Beyond...\n\nAnother brief ridge will build into the region Sunday, giving us \nanother glimpse of sunshine and dry weather. Highs will likely reach \nthe 70s Sunday afternoon. \n\nMidlevel thermal ridging ahead of the next trough and surface cold \nfront will allow temperatures to remain in the 70s on Monday, before \nshowers and storms return to the region. Longer range models \ncontinue to hint at the potential for strong moisture advection into \nthe region with this next system Monday through Wednesday, \npotentially supplemented by moisture from Hurricane Polo off the \nwestern coast of Mexico. NBM probabilities maintain 60-75% chance \nfor at least 1\" of additional rainfall, and 35-50% chance for at \nleast 2\" Monday through Wednesday. Given the well-saturated soil \nconditions and potential for another round of heavy, abundant \nrainfall, areas of flooding may be possible. However, the gradual \ndumping of rain over several days will work to mitigate concerns.  \n\nIn addition to potentially heavy rain, a more unstable and sheared \nair mass could lead to the formation of strong to severe storms, as \nis highlighted by a 5-10% probability in machine learning guidance. \nHowever, exact timing details of potential severe storms remains \nquestionable at this time. \n\nLooking ahead, long range guidance continues to struggle with \nhow quickly the system will pull away to the east. The latest\nrun of the GFS is beginning to come on board with the idea of \nprecipitation and cooler weather persisting through at least \nThursday as a surface low tracks through the Central Plains.",
